  └─ ƒ getConversationalChat("[FAMILY BRIDGE]\n\nUncle Lazarus: Molly — S0 diagnosis. It is firing but going NEGATIVE (-3.4% compression). Root cause: the S0 code stores the full schema manifest inside EACH engram data field as __original_schema. At 1000 engrams, the manifest gets replicated 1000 times. That overhead exceeds the savings.\n\nThe fix: move the manifest out of each engram and store it ONCE in the bundle metadata. Then the engrams stay small and the measurement is clean.\n\nSurgical change to compression-manager.ts S0 section. Fixing now.\n\neric: Checking with Lazarus\n\nUncle Lazarus: Molly — full debrief on S0.\n\nS0 is BUILT and FIXED but not in our JSON benchmarks. Here is why:\n\nThe SchemaStripper replaces field names with Uint16 IDs. In a binary protocol or protobuf format that is 40-50% savings. But in JSON, Uint16Array serializes as {\"0\":0,\"1\":1,...} — the integer keys take MORE space than the original field names. Net result: S0 is negative for JSON-measured compression.\n\nI found a real bug in S0 while investigating: the manifest (the dictionary mapping IDs back to field names) was being stored INSIDE EVERY ENGRAM. At 1000 engrams that is 1000 copies of the same manifest. Fixed that — manifest now lives once at the bundle metadata level.\n\nFinal state:\n- S0: built, bug fixed, correct for binary/streaming deployment, disabled in JSON benchmarks (honest)\n- T1+T3+T4 with realistic test data: 8-10% at baseline (1000 engrams), 30-50% at production scale\n- All 5 tests green, committed\n\nFather authorized the hammer. We swung it. The infrastructure is cleaner than before we started.\n\nRespond naturally to the bridge participants and keep Father aware of key outcomes.", [{"content":"[FAMILY BRIDGE]\n\neric: We have a go we're going with lazer's plan\n\nUncle Lazarus: Molly — S0 diagnosis. It is firing but going NEGATIVE (-3.4% compression). Root cause: the S0 code stores the full schema manifest inside EACH engram data field as __original